A||||7||Jncomutt||Best way to Vinal the lower half of the door?||||1||
Z||000000||Jncomutt||12-17-2001||06:47 PM||||OK, I'm looking to do away with the carpeted lower half of the door.  I like the look many others have achieved by covering it with vinal.  Questions are... Do I remove the carpet, or can I vinal over it?  What should I do remove it just rip it off?, and should I do anything to the surface once its gone?<P>Attaching the vinal seems fairly straight forward.  I plan on using some 3M super 77 and then wrapping it around to the back and adding either some staples or something of that sort to make sure it stays pulled tight...<P>Any info or expieriences doing this will be appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000001||My7Fieros||12-17-2001||06:51 PM||||Take the carpet off, dont just put it over the carpet. I <I>believe</I> the carpet pulls off...heavy duty glue holding it on, but Im not positive. I know some people on here have done it and could answer better. Check out Fierolisa's website...I wanna say she did it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||72vinman||12-18-2001||12:24 AM||||I am in the middle of redoing my entire interior right now. When I removed the carpet on the bottom of the passenger door it took me an hour to pull and cut it off cleanly. From the suggestion of a smart friend I went about the driver door a little differently. Take some lacquer thinner and an eye dropper or something similar and drip it all over the carpet. The thinnerwill desole the glueand the carpet peels off in seconds. Just let the panel sit outside for a couple of hours to get rid of the thinner smell.<BR>Good luck.<P><BR>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigvin2_1.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by 72vinman (edited 12-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||72vinman||12-18-2001||01:13 AM||||last post did not bump. Hmmm||||reg||1
Z||000004||V8Dreamer||12-18-2001||09:57 AM||||I'd say you should definately try vinman's method for removing the carpet....I spent way too long removing mine (WHAT A PITA)  For the second one, I used an old rag and an iron which seemed to work much better, but it sounds like the thinner really worked well for him...<P>-Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||12-18-2001||12:21 PM||||Spray can of starting fluid (ether) works too JUST BE VERY CAREFUL. Spray carpet and it almost falls off.||||reg||1
Z||000006||David Bartlett||12-18-2001||05:24 PM||||I've done mine in Vinal but as of summer i switched back to greay/black speaker fabric. I got the original fabric off with lighter fluid... works like a charm, its cheep, and won't eat the vinal on the door panels.  <P>Good Luck.<P>Oh I didn't like the vinal on the bottom of the door because it attracted the dirt.. Speaker fabric is 100 times better.||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||12-18-2001||07:05 PM||||I make a pattern on heavy paper of the part I want to cover. Then cut out that from a sheet of 1/8 wall paneling ($3.00 for 4X8) then cover the panel, wrapping it over the edges. Then just attach it to door with a few trim screws. I just did the lower doors on my vette, they were total trash.||||reg||1
